   "source": [
    "a) K-fold cross-validated helps estimate the generalisation error of the training procedure, as it uses similar datasets for all k training runs, since (k-2)/(k-1) of the training data stays the same throughout the procedure. As data is heavily shared throughout all of the folds, this does not give an overall assesment of model performance on a blind prediction. \n",
    "\n",
    "b) $s / \\sqrt{k}$ represents positively correlated fold errors, which tend to have a larger variance of the mean than independent ones, thereby representing an overly optimistic error. \n",
    "k = 2: the size of each training set is 0.5 * n, nearly independent folds, however a more pessimistic estimate;\n",
    "k = n : no size bias and no fold randomness, the training sets are basically identical, with highly correlated\n",
    "errors and a high variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "c) If the mean is calculated from the entire training set (or on all of the data), it will be too optimistic, as the included/leaked data will nudge the mean to be closer to the mean of the held out set as it has seen the mean and standard deviation of the set, which it is supposed to blindly predict.\n",
    "\n",
    "A scikit learn Pipeline sets a consistent framework for transformations done on the data, thereby guaranteeing the correct order of operations if implemented correctly.\n",
    "\n",
    "d) The learning rates are assessed based on how well the respective network performed on the test set, which leads to overfitting, as this now no longer guarantees that the network got to this performance through an actual increase of prediction quality. Therefore the reported MSE no longer accurately assesses the MSE of any actual model.\n",
    "This same principle applies to the training of ten different Networks, which are evaluated on the test MSE. The best model no longer represents the best the model with the best blind prediction performance, but rather the model with the best performance on this specific set of test data.\n",
    "\n",
    "e) A network can be overadjusted on a specific set of data, if it is \"overtrained\" by hyperadjusting weights through too many epochs. This will lead to a similar phenomenon as in the above tasks, where the model will show a very low cost on the data it was trained upon, but is too fine tuned to make the best predictions on other data.\n",
    "\n",
    "f) When using e.g. k-fold cross validation to find the penalty parameter $\\lambda$, the model still trains on similar data throughout the different splits, which can lead to some generalisation error, if it is used to blindly predict on unseen training data.\n",
    "Using a $\\lambda$, which is one standard error above the $\\lambda$ with the minimum training error leads to a more regularised model, from which we hope that it holds a lower variance, as it is a more simple overall model.\n",
    "When using this principle on hidden units, we always want to choose the simplest model within one standard deviation of the optimal one. For Neural Networks this usually means choosing the model with the lowest number of hidden nodes / the lowest depth. "
   ]
